26 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444926 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b69e114a-e3b9-5a0f-8073-62abdcb55456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444927 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d781f999-1594-5949-8737-c9a323ba0c9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e3e184e-e905-5d9a-a39c-5d0db9ad9647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444928 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi |}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1-0|}{{[1+(0+1)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1-t)^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37e058b7-fa62-5f7d-a443-fba67e4c6b5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444929 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1-t)^{2},\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|-2(1-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2|}{({(-2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t}|}{({(2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2(0){e}^{(0)}-4{e}^{(0)}|}{({(2(1-(0)))^{2}+({e}^{(0)})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1071 "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}},\\,t>-1.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41bae79a-9c3e-5b74-b3cc-213f841b749e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444930 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})|}{{({1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{{(1+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{4}})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{\\sqrt{{[\\frac{(t+1)^{4}+1}{(t+1)^{4}}]}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{|(t+1)^{3}|\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{(t+1)^{3}\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1071 "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62dfa070-b095-529f-9742-123438c631da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444931 #edb/ref 1074 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1071 "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a0a2c2f-1706-5f21-a00b-714f5830e8bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444932 #edb/ref 1074 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-2t,\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 2{e}^{2t}}{{((-2)^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{{(4+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{8{(1+{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
